demonstrations have been called across  Iran after a second a protester was  executed by the regime Majid Reza  ranavard was detained during  anti-government protests he was  convicted for allegedly stabbing two  security officers to death during  demonstrations human rights activists  say nearly 500 people have now been  killed in the Nationwide protest that  began in the middle of September a  estimate at least a dozen people are  facing the death penalty for taking part  in the protest movement  for more on this let's bring in Cameron  Martini he's a senior lecturer on  international relations at the  University of Sussex in Britain  um Cameron this is the second execute  execution in just uh two weeks after the  alleged offense uh are you surprised by  that quick and very brutal response  good morning Erhard no I'm not this was  expected and the regime has been laying  around for this um this killings which  by all conventions actually are  extrajudicial killings uh the person who  was executed this morning  was only arrested 23 days ago so the  whole process from arrest to execution  took only more a little more than three  weeks he had no access to Independent  lawyer it was a lawyer which was  determined by the by the state and in  fact an Iranian human rights lawyer  claims that given that there was no um  private plaintiff in this case I.E the  whole case was brought to the court by  the government and and actually  processed by the government by Judiciary  itself it's a sort of State killing  um and I should also add that there are  other protesters who are actually being  killed under torture in the prison but  the government then claims they have  been either committing suicide or they  had some other underlying conditions  including for example uh Mr shadman who  is a Kurdish protester who has been  recently the the body has been returned  to the to the family so the killings are  actually in prisons going in the  different ways but as we see and you you  mentioned the protests are already being  called against this and I I don't see  how this uh unbridled violence could  could actually end the protest because  it makes people  excuse me for interrupting you will it  discourage uh protesters if you risk  your life uh and and risk the death  penalty uh will it discourage protesters  I'm not sure it does because people who  go to the streets already prepared for  being actually shot at by Security  Forces something which the government  security troops have done for past three  months I mean you you mentioned in your  clip that in the beginning that more  than 460 people have already been killed  in the in the demonstrations majority of  which have been actually fired up by a  live ammunition during the process so I  think people are aware of  um of the um of the dangers and risks  and they are familiar with the practices  of the Islamic Republic against  opposition and I would also want to say  that in fact the protest or opposition  to these executions have reached the  highest level within the religious  authorities of Shia Islam itself in the  city of hum which is the center of Shia  kind of jurisprudence so the government  is is quite desperate in a way it's it's  a supposedly a show of force but I think  it shows more more desperation and fear  rather than actually Authority and  control  now this is a Grassroots movement there  aren't any any clear leaders How likely  do you think it is that they can bring  out real change even the regime change  in Iran  well I think  um this is both a kind of um a strength  but also potentially in short term at  least a weakness of the movement the  strengths because the regime cannot  easily find the leaders and and deal  with them and therefore bring the the  movement to an end and and weakness  because it it kind of um you know makes  it more difficult to act more  strategically to to be a source of kind  of an interlocutor for International  Community and so on and so f